### Description

An explicit new expression whose class has an `isolated` init method and whose arguments are all isolated is not treated as an isolated expression. As a result it is rejected as the initializer of an isolated module variable. Declaring `isolated Box? box = ();` and assigning `new Box(3)` inside the lock is accepted and prints 4, so the variable's type and the lock body are fine; only the initializer check is wrong. Also rejected the same way: `isolated Box box = new (3);` (implicit new), `isolated Box box = mk();` where `mk` is an `isolated function` returning `Box`, and `<Box>(new Box(3))`.

### Steps to Reproduce

```ballerina
import ballerina/io;

class Box {
    int n;

    isolated function init(int n) {
        self.n = n;
    }
}

isolated Box box = new Box(3);

public function main() {
    lock {
        box.n += 1;
        io:println(box.n); // @output 4
    }
}
```

Run with `go run ./cli/cmd run repro.bal`.

Actual:
```
error[SEMANTIC_ERROR]: initializer of an isolated variable must be an isolated expression
  --> repro.bal:11:20
```

Expected:
```
4
```

### Spec reasoning

§8.7 Module variable declaration: "If a module-init-var-decl includes an isolated-qual, then the variable declared is isolated. In this case, public must not be specified, the binding-pattern in the typed-binding-pattern must be just a variable-name, and the expression must be an isolated expression." There is no restriction on the variable's type.

§6.2.6 Isolated expressions: "The section for each kind of expression will describe any alternative conditions under which that kind of expression is also isolated."

§6.8.2 New expression: "The explicit-new-expr is isolated if the type of the init is isolated and the expression for every argument in the arg-list is isolated." Here `init` is `isolated` and the argument `3` has a readonly static type, so `new Box(3)` is isolated.

Similarly §6.15: "A function-call-expr is isolated if the type of the function being called is isolated and the expression for every argument is isolated", which covers the `mk()` variant.

The maintainer's closing comment on #941 reads §6.2.6 the same way: an explicit-new-expr is isolated either because its static type is an isolated object, or because the §6.8.2 condition holds.

### Affected Version(s)

main @ ea2bcd439

### OS, DB, other environment details and versions

Linux (cloud container), interpreter built from source with `go run ./cli/cmd run`

### Related area

Semantics

### Related issue(s) (optional)

#1076 (new on a class without explicit init rejected in an isolated function) is related, as are #1429 / #1501 (other expression kinds not recognised as isolated). #941 (closed) has the maintainer's reading of §6.2.6.
